[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6968/ZX-Spectrum/Sherlock"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Sherlock</a>
(release date, awards, versions and related titles).

[Fact:wikipedia:Wikipedia]
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherlock_(video_game)"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Sherlock (video game)</a>

[Fact:making:Eighteen Months]
According to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherlock_(video_game)"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Wikipedia</a>, citing The Guardian in 1984, five
programmers worked on Sherlock for 18 months, and a Sherlock Holmes expert
was employed for a year. It was released on 11 September 1984 at
&pound;14.95, written by Philip Mitchell of Beam Software with an inlay by
Lynda Warner (<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6968/ZX-Spectrum/Sherlock"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>).

[Fact:awards:Awards]
Sherlock was a Crash Smash (8/10 in issue 9), scored 10/10 in Personal
Computer News and 9/10 in Sinclair User issue 31, came second for Best
Text/Graphical Adventure in the 1984 Crash Readers Awards, and third in the
Big K readers' poll. Source: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6968/ZX-Spectrum/Sherlock"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:v2:Version 2.0]
A version 2.0 was released in which WAIT UNTIL can be interrupted. The
game also inspired Robin Of Sherlock. Source:
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6968/ZX-Spectrum/Sherlock"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:hobbit:Built On The Hobbit]
Sherlock reuses a great deal of #HOBBIT$6E8B(The Hobbit)'s code; this
disassembly has more than a hundred cross-references to the equivalent code in
The Hobbit, for example #R$A2A2, #R$A59F, #R$A840, #R$AFB3, #R$B4C1 and
#R$D9B7.
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherlock_(video_game)"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Wikipedia</a> also describes Sherlock as
building on The Hobbit.

[Fact:realtime:Time Waits For No One]
The keyboard routine at #R$A536 waits for a key only until a timer runs
out. If you don't press anything in time, #R$D9B7 rubs out whatever you had
typed and the game takes its turn without you, so it carries on in real time
while you think. The timer starts at #N($0BB8,$04,$04) and is reset after
every key.

[Fact:timetables:Timetables]
The characters move about according to timetables. The table at #R$9860
holds eight-byte entries, and is read by routines including #R$A064 and
#R$CC0E.

[Fact:printer:ZX Printer]
Sherlock has its own version of the start of the ROM's
#SKOOLKID$0EF4(COPY_LINE) routine at #R$C403, without the BREAK-key check, and
jumps back into the ROM at #N($0F12,$04,$04) to send each pixel line to the ZX
Printer; #R$C3E4 stops the printer motor at #N$C3FD.
